comments.content:
`renameSync` is unguarded: if the rename throws (e.g., EACCES on Windows when package.json is held by an editor/indexer, EBUSY on Windows Defender, or a transient I/O error), the freshly written `.tmp` is left orphaned on disk and every subsequent run keeps stacking stale `.tmp` files. Also, `JSON.parse` / `readFileSync` for package.json has no try/catch, so any corruption or permission issue surfaces as an opaque stack trace instead of a clear prebuild failure. Wrap the whole read‑parse‑write‑rename block in try/catch, `unlinkSync(tmpPath)` in the catch (best‑effort), and exit with a user‑friendly message. Also use a per‑run unique name (PID or `crypto.randomUUID()`) for `tmpPath` to avoid collisions if a previous crash left a stale `.tmp`.

```js
import { readFileSync, writeFileSync, renameSync, unlinkSync } from 'node:fs';
import { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';
// ...
const tmpPath = `${pkgPath}.${process.pid}.${randomUUID()}.tmp`;
try {
  pkg.version = version;
  writeFileSync(tmpPath, JSON.stringify(pkg, null, 2) + '\n');
  renameSync(tmpPath, pkgPath);
  console.log(`[sync-version] package.json version → ${version}`);
} catch (err) {
  try { unlinkSync(tmpPath); } catch {}
  console.error(`[sync-version] 更新 package.json 失败: ${err.message}`);
  process.exit(1);
}
```

