The boundary detection is fragile. `indexOf('[package]')` matches the first text occurrence of that literal anywhere (e.g., a `[package]` mentioned inside a doc-comment block ahead of the real header would shift everything), and `indexOf('\n[', pkgStart)` truncates the slice at *any* `[` that begins a line inside what is logically still the `[package]` body — notably at `[package.metadata]`, `[package.metadata.docs.rs]`, `[package.metadata.bundle]` etc., which Cargo allows. The consequence: if a project adds `[package.metadata]` *before* the `version = …` line, the regex on line 17 silently fails to match and the script's first error path fires ("未在 src-tauri/Cargo.toml 找到 [package] version"). Either parse the section properly (line-by-line state machine: enter at `[package]`, exit at the next `[anything-else]`), or use a tiny TOML reader like `@iarna/toml`. A minimal line-by-line version:

```js
let version = null;
const lines = cargo.split('\n');
let inPackage = false;
for (const line of lines) {
  const header = line.match(/^\s*\[([^\]]+)\]\s*(?:#.*)?$/);
  if (header) { inPackage = header[1].trim() === 'package'; continue; }
  if (!inPackage) continue;
  const m = line.match(/^\s*version\s*=\s*"([^"]+)"\s*(?:#.*)?$/);
  if (m) { version = m[1]; break; }
}
```

