The global `SSE_WRITERS` mutex is acquired and held across `std::thread::spawn(...)` AND across the `push` into `writers_reg`. The accompanying comment justifies holding the lock to close a TOCTOU window between spawn and register, but this serializes every SSE connect through a single process-wide mutex during an unbounded stack-allocating thread spawn. Under load (or even a moderate burst of reconnects) every new client queues behind every other, defeating the per-connection concurrency the rest of the design tries to preserve. A more scalable pattern: spawn the thread first (passing it a `Weak<Mutex<Vec<...>>>` if you really want self-registration) or hand it an `mpsc::Sender<RegCmd>` that the holder of the lock drains after spawning. As written, the cure is more harmful than the disease — the original TOCTOU is also already defeated by the `alive` weak token and the `stop` flag, since `api_stop` can still signal even writers that aren't yet in the registry.

