Path-token filter uses byte indexing (`tok.as_bytes()[1] == b':'`) on what is actually a `&str`; the surrounding `tok.len() > 2` guards the bound, but the guard is in *bytes* not chars, so a 3-byte multi-byte prefix could shift `:` out of byte position 1 and silently miss a `X:` drive path. Also `trim_matches(|c| matches!(c, '*'|'_'|'-'|'>'|'#'))` runs on every split segment — a token like `**/foo.docx` collapses to `/foo.docx`, masking the original wrapping. The second filter relies on the first (ext) having rejected empty strings; if the order ever changes, `tok.as_bytes()[1]` on an empty/all-trimmed token will panic.

