Mutex poisoning recovery silently hides corrupted state. The `unwrap_or_else(|e| e.into_inner())` pattern is used at every lock acquisition (production `emit_proposals` plus the four `emitted_map()` calls inside tests and the `EMIT_TEST_LOCK` acquisition). If any thread panics while holding the guard mid-mutation (e.g. inside `g.retain` / `g.insert`), the `HashMap<String, i64>` can be left in an inconsistent state (partial retain, half-applied insert), and the next caller will silently continue using it — silently breaking the 24h dedup contract and allowing the same proposal to be emitted multiple times across process lifetime. `e.into_inner()` after a panic is only sound when the critical section cannot leave the protected value in a torn state; here, an iterator-based `retain` + `insert` in the same scope does not give that guarantee. Either drop the recovery (poison the mutex — callers will see a clear panic and a restart fixes state) or use a recovery strategy that re-validates the map.

```rust
let mut g = emitted_map().lock().unwrap_or_else(|e| {
    eprintln!("[mutex_poisoned] evolution::emit::emitted_map: {e:?}");
    e.into_inner()
});
```

