comments.content:
Capacity invariant is not enforced atomically. `evict_if_needed` reads `COUNT(*)` and then returns; the subsequent `INSERT` in `insert_new` is the only thing that grows the table. Two connections that both observe `count < capacity` between their transactions (or one observing the stale count, the other racing the DELETE+INSERT pair from a sibling transaction) can both INSERT and push the table past `MAX_MEM_ITEMS`. The file comment says locking lives in `memory/mod.rs`, but every read-modify-write path here relies on that assumption and there is no guard inside the store (no `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`, no advisory lock, no `INSERT ... WHERE (SELECT COUNT(*)) < capacity`). Consider upgrading to `unchecked_transaction` with `TransactionBehavior::Immediate`, or wrapping the COUNT+DELETE+INSERT in a single `INSERT ... SELECT` so the cap is checked in the same write statement.

