`move_entry` discards the original rename error (`if let Err(_e)`) and unconditionally falls back to copy+remove on ANY rename failure. This is not just EXDEV: permission denial, missing source, locked target, or transient I/O errors all trigger the same fallback. As a result, the function may try to `fs::copy`/`fs::remove_*` against a `src` that has already disappeared or that the user is not allowed to touch. Bind the error (`let e = fs::rename(...)`) and branch on `e.kind()` (e.g. `ErrorKind::CrossesDevices` / EXDEV on Unix / `ERROR_NOT_SAME_DEVICE` on Windows) so non-EXDEV failures propagate with their original cause instead of masquerading as cross-volume copy errors.

```rust
match fs::rename(src, dst) {
    Ok(()) => Ok(()),
    Err(e) if is_cross_volume(&e) => { /* fallback */ }
    Err(e) => return Err(format!("rename failed: {e}")),
}
```

