The post-rename chmod is silently swallowed with `let _ = ...`, but `set_permissions` can legitimately fail on filesystems that don't support POSIX mode bits (FAT/exFAT external drives, some container overlays, certain NFS mounts, Windows ACL handling) — leaving the PII JSONL with the user's default umask (often 0644 → world-readable). Either surface the error (so a CI/eval run refuses to claim "exported with 0600" when it didn't), or pre-create the file with restrictive perms via `OpenOptions::new().create_new(true).mode(0o600).write(true).open(...)`, then atomic-rename over it — that way the contents and the ACL are inseparable and the chmod failure becomes a write failure.

```rust
#[cfg(unix)]
use std::os::unix::fs::OpenOptionsExt;

#[cfg(unix)]
{
    use std::io::Write;
    let mut f = std::fs::OpenOptions::new()
        .write(true).create_new(true).mode(0o600).open(&out_path)
        .unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!("创建 0600 采样文件失败：{e}"));
    f.write_all(format!("{}\n", lines.join("\n")).as_bytes())
        .and_then(|_| f.sync_all())
        .unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!("写采样文件失败：{e}"));
}
#[cfg(not(unix))]
std::fs::write(&out_path, format!("{}\n", lines.join("\n")))
    .unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!("写采样文件失败：{e}"));
```

