The `.tmp` file is written with default perms (no chmod before `write`), so if the test is killed between `write` and `rename` the `.tmp` sits at default umask with the full PII payload on disk for the OS to clean up whenever. The code-comment claims "中断不留半截采样文件" but in practice a `.tmp` half lives. Write the body to an `mkstemp`-style temp file created with `O_EXCL` + 0600, then `rename` — even if the process is killed, the temp is owner-only from the moment bytes land.

