comments.content:
fetchBingDaily returns the raw invoke() promise with no error normalization, violating the async error-handling standard. A failed backend call (network/IPC failure, deserialization error, backend panic) will surface as an opaque Tauri rejection to the UI. Wrap with try/catch or attach a .catch that converts errors into a user-friendly message (or at minimum log + rethrow with context) so the consumer widget can render a friendly fallback instead of an unhandled rejection.

```ts
export async function fetchBingDaily(): Promise<BingDaily> {
  try {
    return await invoke<BingDaily>("bing_daily");
  } catch (err) {
    throw new Error(`Failed to load Bing daily image: ${(err as Error)?.message ?? err}`);
  }
}
```

