comments.content:
`act()` warning filter relies on a substring regex against the raw `console.error` first argument. It silently drops any error whose message happens to contain "not wrapped in act(" (false positives), but more importantly it never inspects `args[1..]`, which is where React 19 prints the offending stack/component name (`console.error("not wrapped in act(...)", %s, componentName, stack)`). A more robust filter would match the React-emitted prefix using React's own convention (the message usually starts with `Warning:` and contains `not wrapped in act`) and log only the suppressed count, not the raw args. Also consider wrapping async component interactions in `await act(...)` rather than globally mutating logging, which can mask real flakiness that legitimately surfaces.

Existing code:
```js
  vi.spyOn(console, "error").mockImplementation((...args: unknown[]) => {
    const msg = typeof args[0] === "string" ? args[0] : "";
    if (/not wrapped in act\(/.test(msg)) return;
    realConsoleError(...args);
  });
```

