Unsafe `as unknown as` cast bypasses type checking: after only validating `questionId`, the entire payload is force-cast to `WorkflowQuestionPayload` and then `q.options`, `q.why`, `q.nodeTitle`, `q.assumption` are read directly. A malformed backend payload (e.g. `options` being an object, `why` being a number) will either render `undefined` text or throw at runtime when iterating. Either narrow the cast with explicit per-field guards, or at minimum tighten the read sites — `q.why`/`q.nodeTitle`/`q.assumption` should be checked with `typeof === "string"` before being rendered.

Existing context:
```tsx
function workflowQuestion(n: NotificationItem): WorkflowQuestionPayload | null {
  const p = n.payload;
  if (!p || typeof p.questionId !== "string") return null;
  return p as unknown as WorkflowQuestionPayload;
}
```

comments.suggestion_code:
function workflowQuestion(n: NotificationItem): WorkflowQuestionPayload | null {
  const p = n.payload;
  if (!p || typeof p.questionId !== "string") return null;
  if (typeof p.why !== "string" && p.why !== undefined) return null;
  if (typeof p.nodeTitle !== "string" && p.nodeTitle !== undefined) return null;
  if (typeof p.assumption !== "string" && p.assumption !== undefined) return null;
  if (p.options !== undefined && !Array.isArray(p.options)) return null;
  return p as unknown as WorkflowQuestionPayload;
}

